1. Why does a &str carry a length?
   &s[..1] = "h"      offset Some(0)   len 1
   &s[..6] = "héllo"  offset Some(0)   len 6
   Same pointer, different text. The pointer says where a view starts;
   only the length says where it stops. A C char * has no length word,
   so strlen walks the bytes until it finds a 0.

2. After `let t = s;`, which bytes moved?
   buffer address changed: false
   The three words were copied into t and s stopped being usable.
   The 12 bytes on the heap stayed where they were.

3. Why is &s[1..2] on "héllo" a panic and not a compile error?
   cut("hello", 1, 2) = "e"
   cut("héllo", 1, 2) panicked: end byte index 2 is not a char boundary; it is inside 'é' (bytes 1..3 of string)
   One function, compiled once. Its bounds are two usize values, and
   whether byte 2 starts a character depends on the bytes, which exist
   only when the program runs.
   "héllo".get(1..2) = None   the same question, without the panic
